---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Still from '../../components/Still.astro';
import AnonPlate from '../../components/AnonPlate.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'SED — Sistema de Evaluación Docente',
  applicationCategory: 'EducationalApplication',
  description:
    'Anonymous teacher-evaluation platform for a public university in Mexico: opaque respondent credentials, immutable submissions, Django + React, with 2,500+ automated tests.',
  operatingSystem: 'Web',
  creator: { '@id': `${site.url}/#person` },
  url: site.sedLiveUrl,
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SED — Teacher Evaluation System"
  description="How I built an anonymous, tamper-evident teacher-evaluation platform for a public Mexican university: Django, React 19, opaque credentials, and 2,500+ automated tests."
  path="/work/sed/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="file-kicker">Case study · Institutional platform</p>
      <h1 class="display" style="max-width:18ch">
        Teacher evaluations, made anonymous.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        SED is how a public university in Mexico evaluates its teaching.
        Students, teachers, and staff answer every term, and the system never
        stores a link between an answer and the person who gave it.
      </p>
      <p class="chips" style="color:var(--ink-soft)">
        <span>Django + DRF</span>
        <span>React 19</span>
        <span>PostgreSQL</span>
        <span>Redis + Celery</span>
        <span>Docker / AWS</span>
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <a class="btn btn--solid" href={site.sedLiveUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Open the live system
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-bottom:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Still
        product
        src="/images/sed/landing.webp"
        alt="Live SED landing page with two entries: evaluate teachers, or open the admin panel."
        width={1400}
        height={953}
        caption="Live landing on the current deployment. Two audiences, one system."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-bottom:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem)">
    <div class="wrap media-split" style="margin-top:0">
      <Still
        product
        src="/images/sed/eval.webp"
        alt="Respondent screen: enter an institutional access code to start an anonymous evaluation."
        width={1400}
        height={875}
        caption="Respondent entry. One opaque code. No account."
      />
      <div>
        <p class="file-kicker">By construction</p>
        <h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:0.4rem">How anonymity is built</h2>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:0.9rem 0 1.25rem; max-width:46ch">
          You never create an account. And once your answer is in, nobody
          can edit it, not even an admin.
        </p>
        <AnonPlate />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-bottom:clamp(3rem,6vw,4rem)">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Picture this: you're a student and the university asks you to rate
        your teacher. Would you be honest if you weren't sure it was
        anonymous? Me neither. That was the problem with the old system.
        People couldn't be sure their answers were anonymous, so fewer of them
        answered, and fewer answered honestly. The new one had to be anonymous
        <em>by construction</em>, hold up when everyone answers at the end of
        the term, and give administrators reports they can audit.
      </p>

      <h2>What I built</h2>
      <p>
        One platform with two separate sides. An admin console for catalogs,
        teaching assignments, survey instruments, credentials, and analytics.
        And a respondent flow that only needs one opaque code and leaves no
        trail back to you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap media-split" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <Still
        product
        src="/images/sed/dashboard.webp"
        alt="Coordinator dashboard with period metrics, category averages, and academy results."
        width={1400}
        height={784}
        caption="Coordinator dashboard. Burner period data from a local capture."
      />
      <Still
        product
        src="/images/sed/instruments.webp"
        alt="Instrument management screen with a 360 evaluation package and create-instrument actions."
        width={1400}
        height={784}
        caption="Survey instruments and 360 packages. Burner labels left visible."
      />
    </div>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Opaque credentials:</strong> you trade a token from the
          server for an anonymous session. No name, no account, no way to link
          an answer to a person.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Answers that can't change:</strong> once an evaluation is
          sent, nobody can edit or delete it. The data the university reports
          on is exactly the data that was collected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Survey engine:</strong> configurable instruments with matrix
          questions, Likert scales, open text, theory/practical classification
          questions. Each instrument follows a strict lifecycle:
          draft → published → closed, with soft-deletes instead of hard
          erasure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Analytics &amp; reports:</strong> per-professor and
          per-unit reports for institutional decision-making.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Engineering standards</h2>
      <p>Every stage has checks, from the first commit to the release:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1,750+ backend tests</strong> and <strong>830+ frontend tests</strong>, run in CI on every change.</li>
        <li><strong>Strict bundle budgets:</strong> every JavaScript chunk is measured and capped for slower devices and campus Wi-Fi.</li>
        <li><strong>Ongoing security review:</strong> authentication boundaries, sessions, and data storage are reviewed throughout development. Findings are tracked until they are resolved.</li>
        <li><strong>Observability built in:</strong> health checks, request tracing, and metrics wired to monitoring from day one.</li>
        <li><strong>Reproducible deployment:</strong> Docker images built in CI, promoted through environments, released to AWS with an audited pipeline.</li>
      </ul>

      <blockquote>
        The requirement was never just "collect evaluations." It was:
        prove to thousands of students that their voice is safe.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Result</h2>
      <p>
        The university can run every evaluation period on it. It never
        collects who you are, answers can't be changed once they're sent, and
        automated tests guard the main flows. There's also architecture
        documentation, so the next engineer doesn't depend on someone
        remembering how it all works.
      </p>
      <p>
        The current deployment is live at
        <a class="link-arrow" href={site.sedLiveUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{site.sedLiveUrl.replace('https://', '')}</a>.
        Admin and evaluation entry require institutional credentials.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-bottom:clamp(4rem,8vw,6rem)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="folder folder--orchid">
        <h2 class="h2">Building something that handles sensitive data?</h2>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1rem">
          Tell me what it has to protect and who's going to use it. We'll
          figure out the rest together.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn--solid" style="margin-top:1.75rem" href="/contact/">Talk about your project</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
